@chapter 9 | Economic Planning and NITI Aayog

@intro
This chapter covers **planning in India**: its constitutional place, the **Planning Commission** and **National Development Council**, pre-independence plans, the **twelve Five Year Plans** (their models, slogans, targets and periods), plan holidays and the **rolling plan**, and the change to **NITI Aayog** in 2015. It contains **{pyqs} PYQs and {ones} one-liners**.
@end

@section 9.1 | Planning Bodies

@q 7:1-4

@q 7:5

@src1 7:2
@src1 7:3
@one
The Planning Commission in India was created by
@ans a Cabinet Resolution
@end

@one U.P.P.C.S. (Mains) 2014
Planning Commission of India is a
@ans Quasi-Political body
@end

@table
Body | Fact
**Economic and social planning** | **Concurrent List** (Entry 20)
**Planning Commission** | **15 March 1950**, by a **Cabinet resolution**; non-constitutional, advisory; PM as chairman; Ashok Chanda called it the "**economic cabinet**"; abolished **1 January 2015**
**National Development Council** | **6 August 1952**; PM, Union ministers, all **Chief Ministers**; gave **final approval to Five Year Plans**
**NITI Aayog** (National Institution for Transforming India) | **1 January 2015**; PM as chairman, full-time **Vice-Chairman**; **cooperative federalism**; Governing Council of all CMs; think tank, no power to allot funds
NITI Vice-Chairmen | **Arvind Panagariya** (first), Rajiv Kumar, **Suman Bery** (since 2022)
**DMEO** | Development Monitoring and Evaluation Office, attached to **NITI Aayog**
@end

@q 7:6-10

NITI Aayog is the **nodal body for the SDGs** and publishes the **SDG India Index**, the **India Innovation Index** (Karnataka has topped every edition), the **Export Preparedness Index** and the **Fiscal Health Index**. Its first Fiscal Health Index (2025) ranked **Odisha** first.

@section 9.2 | Ideas Behind Planning

@q 7:11-15

@table
Pre-independence plan | Year | By
Planned Economy for India | 1934 | **M. Visvesvaraya**
National Planning Committee | 1938 | **Congress, under Nehru** (Subhas Chandra Bose as Congress president)
**Bombay Plan** | **1944** | Eight industrialists (J.R.D. Tata, G.D. Birla and others)
**Gandhian Plan** | **1944** | **Shriman Narayan Agarwal**
**People's Plan** | 1945 | **M.N. Roy**
Sarvodaya Plan | 1950 | **Jayaprakash Narayan**
@end

The **Gadgil formula** (1969) shared Central plan assistance among states, giving the most weight to **population** (60%); the **Gadgil-Mukherjee** revision (1991) kept population at 60%. Gandhi favoured a decentralised, village-based economy and **opposed central planning**.

@q 7:16

The **rolling plan** idea came from **Gunnar Myrdal** (*Indian Economic Planning in its Broader Setting*). It was used by the **Janata government** from **1978** to 1980, after it ended the Fifth Plan a year early.

